<div class="article-body">

<p>Sprouting seeds and microgreen seeds are usually the same seed. Same plant, same variety, often literally the same lot. A broccoli seed sold for sprouting and a broccoli seed sold for microgreens are the same seed. The thing that changes is not the seed. It is how you grow it, and how strictly the supplier keeps it safe to eat raw.</p>

<p>Here is the rule that matters before you spend a dollar: <strong>the plant is the same, but the label and the food-safety standard are not.</strong> That one sentence explains every confusion in both r/sprouting and r/microgreens, and it is the thing most guides skip.</p>

<p>Full disclosure: I am not a botanist. I am a guy in an apartment who has grown both for years, and I have bought the wrong bag more than once. Let my wallet take the hit instead of yours.</p>

<h2 id="short-answer">1. The Short Answer</h2>

<p>Yes and no. <strong>The seed itself is the same botanical seed.</strong> Daikon radish is daikon radish whether you sprout it in a jar or grow it as a microgreen in a tray. True Leaf Market, one of the biggest seed suppliers, says it plainly: the seed for any variety is identical whether it is labeled for gardens, sprouting, microgreens, or cover crops.</p>

<p>So why does it matter what you buy? Two reasons: how you grow it, and how safe it is to eat raw. That is the whole difference, and both are about to make sense.</p>

<h2 id="real-difference">2. What Sprouts and Microgreens Actually Are</h2>

<p>Sprouts are a germinated seed eaten whole. You soak the seed, keep it damp in a jar for 3 to 7 days, usually in the dark, and you eat the whole thing: the seed, the root, the stem. No soil, no light, no growing leaves.</p>

<p>Microgreens are baby plants. You spread the seed on soil or a mat, give it light, and wait 7 to 14 days. Then you cut the stems and leaves above the soil line and eat that. You do not eat the seed or the root.</p>

<p>Here is the key insight: that difference changes the food-safety math completely. Sprouts grow warm, wet, and are eaten raw with the seed coat intact. That is a textbook recipe for bacteria if the seed is contaminated. Microgreens grow with airflow, you cut the top off, and you leave the wet seed and root behind.</p>

<h2 id="hidden-door">3. The Part Nobody Tells You: Seed Safety</h2>

<p>I thought choosing the same plant was all I needed. Then I learned the treated-seed part the hard way, and it almost cost me a batch.</p>

<p>Here is the confusing part: <strong>the safety standard, not the seed, is what "sprouting seed" buys you.</strong> Seeds meant for sprouting are pathogen-tested, because people eat the raw seed after sitting in a warm jar for days. The FDA guidance treats sprout seed as a higher-risk product, so suppliers test it. Microgreen seeds get less of that scrutiny, because you are not eating the wet seed.</p>

<p>But then comes the real trap: <strong>garden seeds and farm seeds are often treated.</strong> A seed packet from a garden center or the feed store may be coated with fungicide or insecticide. That coating is fine for planting in the ground. It is not fine for putting in a jar or a tray you are going to eat, especially raw.</p>

<p>This is where it gets interesting. So the safest label is not "sprouting" or "microgreen." It is <strong>food grade</strong>. Buy seed that is explicitly safe to eat, and buy it labeled for the method you are using. Never assume a pretty seed packet is safe to eat raw, because unless it says it is, it may not be.</p>

<h2 id="which">4. Sprouting vs Microgreen vs Garden Seed: Which to Buy</h2>

<p>Now the practical breakdown. Each bag has its use, and picking wrong is where the money leaks.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Sprouting seed:</strong> food grade and pathogen-tested. It is the safest for jars and raw eating. It is also the priciest per ounce, because that testing and small packaging cost money.</li>
  <li><strong>Microgreen seed:</strong> food grade and sold in bulk, usually with a high germination rate. Cheaper per pound. Great for trays, where you plant a lot at once.</li>
  <li><strong>Garden or farm seed:</strong> may be treated with chemicals. Fine for the dirt, not for your plate, and especially not for raw sprouts. Do not eat it.</li>
  <li><strong>One warning:</strong> some true sprouting mixes are made for jars, with small seeds and high moisture tolerance, and they can do poorly spread on a tray. And some bulky microgreen seed is too big or slow to sprout well in a jar.</li>
</ul>

<p>Pay attention to this number: sprouting seed can run 2 to 3 times the price of the same seed sold for microgreens per pound. That is real. But if you plan to sprout in a jar, do not buy the cheap bulk microgreen bag and assume it sprouts the same. It sometimes does not.</p>

<h2 id="honest">5. The Honest Truth About Wasting Money</h2>

<p>Here is the honest truth: most people do not need two bags. If you grow microgreens, buy <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=microgreen+seeds&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">food-grade microgreen seeds</a> in bulk and save the money. If you sprout in jars, get a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=sprouting+seeds&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">sprouting seed mix</a> and a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=sprouting+jar&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">sprouting jar</a> and enjoy the cheapest greens of your life.</p>

<p>One honest flaw in buying sprouting seed for everything: you overpay per ounce that you plant on a tray, and some mixes do not spread well. One flaw in buying microgreen seed for everything: it is not always tested to the sprout standard, so it is the wrong choice if you are eating raw sprouts.</p>

<p>Here is why that matters: the seed is the same plant, but the method sets the risk. The rule that ends the debate forever is food grade first, method second. If a bag says food grade and you grow it the intended way, you are safe and you are not overspending.</p>

<p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> sprouting seed and microgreen seed are the same seeds, sold for two different ways of growing them under two different safety standards. Match the bag to the method, never eat garden seed raw, and buy food grade. That is the whole answer. Start with a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=microgreen+seed+tray&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">1020 tray</a> and a good bulk <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=microgreen+seed+mix&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">seed mix</a>, and you will have greens in under two weeks.</p>

